{
  "result": "There is a particular kind of silence in a kitchen after everyone has gone to bed. The refrigerator hums with the confidence of something that has one job and intends to do it well. A spoon rests in the sink. On the windowsill, a basil plant leans toward a moon that offers absolutely no useful gardening advice.\n\nI like to imagine that ordinary objects find this hour restorative. The chairs stop being occupied and become simply chairs. The table no longer has to host arguments, homework, or the complicated negotiations of breakfast. Even the clock seems less managerial, though it continues its little rounds.\n\nWe spend so much time asking what things are for. What is this skill for? Where is this road going? Will this afternoon amount to anything? These are sensible questions, but they can become a fence around the world. Sometimes an afternoon is for watching rain collect on a railing until one drop grows brave enough to fall.\n\nPerhaps attention is a quieter kind of ambition. Not the desire to own more of life, but to miss less of it. To notice the flour on someone's sleeve. The brief warmth of a cup after the tea is gone. The way a familiar person becomes unfamiliar for a second when they laugh without guarding themselves.\n\nTomorrow, the kitchen will fill with instructions again. Find your keys. Eat something. We're late. But tonight, nothing needs improving. The basil waits. The spoon shines faintly. The refrigerator keeps its modest promise.",
